The `--render-js` or more accurately `--execute-js` or `--execute=js` flag is not a standard option in the traditional `wget` command. However, there is a variation of `wget` called `wget2` or `wget` with the `--execute` option that can execute JavaScript.

If you're using `wget2`, you can use the `--execute=js` flag to execute JavaScript. Here's an example:

```bash
wget2 --execute=js https://example.com
```

However, if you're using the traditional `wget`, it does not support executing JavaScript out of the box.

Alternatively, you can use `curl` with a tool like `jsdom` or `puppeteer` to execute JavaScript and fetch the rendered HTML.

Here's an example using `curl` and `jsdom`:

```bash
curl https://example.com | jsdom
```

And here's an example using `puppeteer`:

```javascript
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://example.com');
  const html = await page.content();
  console.log(html);
  await browser.close();
})();
```

Please note that these are more complex solutions and might require additional setup and dependencies.

If you're looking for a simple way to fetch the rendered HTML of a webpage, you might want to consider using a dedicated tool like `puppeteer` or `playwright` instead of `wget`.

